WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:06.816
This is Chapter One, Episode 3, Part Two.

3
00:00:07.366 --> 00:00:09.479
Now the world goes to war.

4
00:00:09.428 --> 00:00:15.254
1914 to 18 saw the First World War rage between Europe's great powers.

5
00:00:15.253 --> 00:00:18.241
Denmark avoided becoming directly involved.

6
00:00:18.241 --> 00:00:27.353
The years that followed were marked by a highly deadly influenza epidemic, and by political unrest in both Denmark and the rest of the world.

7
00:00:27.353 --> 00:00:30.678
Many European countries developed into dictatorships.

8
00:00:30.678 --> 00:00:35.404
In the 1930s, the world was in deep economic crisis.

9
00:00:35.404 --> 00:00:38.541
In Denmark too, unemployment grew sharply.

10
00:00:38.541 --> 00:00:44.216
That triggered many conflicts on the labour market and gave the Social Democrats great support.

11
00:00:44.216 --> 00:00:53.706
It also motivated the old parties to find solutions together, which helped ensure that anti-democratic ideologies never really gained support in Denmark.

12
00:00:54.256 --> 00:00:57.919
In 1914 the First World War broke out.

13
00:00:57.868 --> 00:01:02.868
On one side stood Germany, Austria-Hungary and the Ottoman Empire.

14
00:01:02.868 --> 00:01:08.969
On the other side stood France, Russia, Great Britain, Italy, and later also the USA.

15
00:01:08.969 --> 00:01:15.043
Neutral Denmark lay geographically close to the fronts of the war, but was not directly affected.

16
00:01:15.043 --> 00:01:20.844
Denmark was neutral, and was never occupied by German troops in the First World War.

17
00:01:20.844 --> 00:01:27.900
The German occupation of Denmark came only in the Second World War, from 1940 to 1945.

18
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:36.150
After pressure from Germany, Denmark did, in 1914, choose to lay mines in some of the Danish waters.

19
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:43.463
Germany's purpose was to stop British warships from sailing into the Baltic Sea and thereby threatening Germany from there.

20
00:01:43.463 --> 00:01:48.425
But the minefields could also stop German ships from sailing through the Danish straits.

21
00:01:48.425 --> 00:02:03.450
One Danish population group became particularly involved in the First World War: the Danish-minded Schleswigers south of the border, who had become German citizens after Denmark's loss of Schleswig in the Second Schleswig War in 1864.

22
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:10.758
In total, about six thousand Danish South Jutlanders died in German war service during the First World War.

23
00:02:11.308 --> 00:02:16.395
The Danish colonies in the West Indies also came into focus because of the war.

24
00:02:16.345 --> 00:02:27.583
The USA feared that Germany might occupy the Danish West Indies, and therefore offered the Danish government in 1915 to buy Saint Thomas, Saint Croix and Saint John.

25
00:02:27.583 --> 00:02:34.845
After a referendum in Denmark, the islands were sold in 1917 for twenty-five million dollars.

26
00:02:34.845 --> 00:02:42.432
In return for the sale, the US government recognised that Greenland belonged fully and completely to Denmark.

27
00:02:42.982 --> 00:02:54.482
Toward the end of the war, in 1918, the world and Denmark were hit by a very aggressive, contagious and deadly influenza virus, which got the name the Spanish Flu.

28
00:02:54.432 --> 00:03:01.644
The disease did not originate in Spain at all, but got its name because it was Spanish newspapers that first wrote about it.

29
00:03:01.644 --> 00:03:15.618
Because Denmark did not take part directly in the First World War, the country was better equipped to fight the disease, with a population that was better nourished and a health service that was less burdened than those of the many warring countries.

30
00:03:16.168 --> 00:03:19.805
Even so, the Danish authorities could not do much.

31
00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:24.443
Little was known about the disease, and for example no vaccine could be developed.

32
00:03:24.443 --> 00:03:32.893
Many influenza patients also got pneumonia on top, which, before penicillin was discovered in 1928, they often died of.

33
00:03:32.893 --> 00:03:47.268
The Danish Health Authority, Sundhedsstyrelsen, tried through leaflets to give the population advice on, among other things, good hygiene and on avoiding contact with each other, and some schools and entertainment venues closed down.

34
00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:55.205
But all this was not enough to get the disease under control, and it hit the country in several waves in the period 1918 to 20.

35
00:03:55.205 --> 00:04:00.255
A special feature of the Spanish Flu is that it largely killed relatively young people.

36
00:04:00.255 --> 00:04:03.805
Most of the dead were between twenty and forty years old.

37
00:04:03.805 --> 00:04:10.260
In total, the disease took up to fifty million lives, including about fifteen thousand in Denmark.

38
00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.860
Now to the reunification.

39
00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.422
Germany lost the First World War.

40
00:04:15.422 --> 00:04:21.335
This made it possible for Denmark to demand back the primarily Danish-minded areas of Schleswig.

41
00:04:21.335 --> 00:04:32.572
In 1920, the population in North Schleswig and Central Schleswig voted, in two different referendums, on whether it wished to belong to Germany or Denmark.

42
00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:36.735
The vote in North Schleswig took place in February 1920.

43
00:04:36.735 --> 00:04:41.647
A large majority voted for North Schleswig to be reunited with Denmark.

44
00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:53.910
At the vote in March 1920 in Central Schleswig, where Flensburg was the largest town, a large majority on the other hand decided that the area should remain under Germany.

45
00:04:54.460 --> 00:05:03.797
On the fifteenth of June, 1920, the border was formally moved south from the Kongeå river to the present border between Denmark and Germany.

46
00:05:03.747 --> 00:05:10.685
North Schleswig, now called Sønderjylland, South Jutland, was thereby reunited with Denmark.

47
00:05:10.685 --> 00:05:15.947
The return of South Jutland to Denmark is called the Reunification, Genforeningen.

48
00:05:15.947 --> 00:05:27.160
But there is still a German minority, in 2020 about fifteen thousand, on the Danish side of the border, and a Danish minority, in 2020 about fifty thousand, on the German side.

49
00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:36.597
The rights of these minorities were laid down in 1955 in the Copenhagen-Bonn Declarations, which has secured peaceful development in the border region.

50
00:05:36.597 --> 00:05:40.272
Christian the Tenth rode across the border in 1920.

51
00:05:40.822 --> 00:05:52.072
Shortly before the Reunification, Denmark experienced, in connection with the Easter Crisis, Påskekrisen, its most serious constitutional crisis in recent times.

52
00:05:52.022 --> 00:06:00.472
Before that, King Christian the Tenth, who reigned from nineteen twelve to forty-seven, and the government had for a long time disagreed.

53
00:06:00.472 --> 00:06:05.909
It was especially the disagreement about the new border to Germany that triggered the Easter Crisis.

54
00:06:05.909 --> 00:06:13.872
The king thought the government, despite the referendum results, should try to get more of Schleswig back to Denmark than just North Schleswig.

55
00:06:13.872 --> 00:06:15.847
The government rejected this.

56
00:06:15.847 --> 00:06:21.922
At Easter 1920, Christian the Tenth dismissed the government because of this disagreement.

57
00:06:21.922 --> 00:06:26.586
This happened even though the government did not have a majority against it in the Folketing.

58
00:06:27.136 --> 00:06:37.173
The king's conduct during the Easter Crisis in 1920 triggered big demonstrations and political protests, and was described by parts of the daily press as a coup.

59
00:06:37.123 --> 00:06:41.048
More people began to talk about abolishing the monarchy altogether.

60
00:06:41.048 --> 00:06:51.148
The crisis blew over when the king and the political leaders, after intense negotiations, agreed to appoint a new temporary government and call a new election.

61
00:06:51.148 --> 00:06:56.148
Since then, royal power has not interfered directly in politics.

62
00:06:56.698 --> 00:06:59.073
Finally, the crisis years.

63
00:06:59.023 --> 00:07:07.210
In 1917 the Bolsheviks, the communists, took power in Russia, which was transformed into the Soviet Union.

64
00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:21.698
Fascism and Nazism gained ground in, for example, Italy in 1922 and in Germany in 1933, and many other European countries got authoritarian regimes during the 1920s and 1930s.

65
00:07:21.698 --> 00:07:25.298
All of it expressed a movement in a non-democratic direction.

66
00:07:25.298 --> 00:07:29.262
But in Denmark, it succeeded in holding on to democracy.

67
00:07:29.812 --> 00:07:35.724
At the same time, a very serious international economic event took place in this period.

68
00:07:35.674 --> 00:07:40.774
It was the stock market crash on Wall Street in New York in October 1929.

69
00:07:40.774 --> 00:07:46.324
Share prices fell sharply, and the economic crisis spread to the rest of the world.

70
00:07:46.324 --> 00:07:52.562
In Denmark, the country's biggest industry, agriculture, was especially hard hit.

71
00:07:52.562 --> 00:07:55.674
The prices of grain and meat fell dramatically.

72
00:07:55.674 --> 00:08:00.624
Thousands of farmers were forced to sell their farms because they did not earn enough money.

73
00:08:00.624 --> 00:08:06.768
Unemployment rose sharply, and reached over thirty percent at the beginning of the 1930s.

74
00:08:07.318 --> 00:08:16.943
In 1924, the cigar sorter Thorvald Stauning had become Denmark's first Social Democratic prime minister, in the year 1924.

75
00:08:16.893 --> 00:08:24.755
This government, which lasted two years, until 1926, also included the country's first female minister.

76
00:08:24.755 --> 00:08:28.743
Her name was Nina Bang, and she was minister of education.

77
00:08:28.743 --> 00:08:35.430
So both the first Social Democratic prime minister and the first female minister date from 1924.

78
00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:43.368
In 1929, the Social Democrats formed a government again, this time together with the Social Liberal Party.

79
00:08:43.368 --> 00:08:47.530
The government tried to ease the crisis through various interventions.

80
00:08:47.530 --> 00:09:01.393
On the thirtieth of January, 1933, coincidentally the same day Adolf Hitler became chancellor in Germany, the government and Venstre made the so-called Kanslergade Agreement, Kanslergadeforliget.

81
00:09:01.393 --> 00:09:09.162
It got its name because the decisive negotiations took place at Prime Minister Stauning's residence in Kanslergade in Copenhagen.

82
00:09:09.712 --> 00:09:16.612
The Kanslergade Agreement was a far-reaching settlement that took into account the interests of both employers and workers.

83
00:09:16.562 --> 00:09:21.737
The agreement prevented lower wages for the workers, but banned strikes for one year.

84
00:09:21.737 --> 00:09:27.387
It was also necessary to support agriculture, which was hard hit by the economic crisis.

85
00:09:27.387 --> 00:09:32.637
The agreement made it easier for farmers to borrow money, so they avoided going bankrupt.

86
00:09:32.637 --> 00:09:38.537
At the same time, the Danish krone was devalued, which means its value was lowered.

87
00:09:38.537 --> 00:09:42.348
This made it easier to sell agricultural goods to Great Britain.

88
00:09:42.898 --> 00:09:59.742
And as part of the agreement, a so-called social reform was also carried out, which extended the population's social rights and created more uniform rules for when the individual citizen could get financial help from the public, for example as a result of unemployment or illness.

89
00:10:00.292 --> 00:10:02.542
Here's your recap for Episode Three.

90
00:10:02.492 --> 00:10:07.567
Venstre is the farmers' party, and the Social Democrats are the workers' party.

91
00:10:07.567 --> 00:10:16.104
In 1924, Stauning became the first Social Democratic prime minister, and Nina Bang the first female minister.

92
00:10:16.104 --> 00:10:22.667
Stauning led governments again from 1929, through the crisis of the 1930s.

93
00:10:22.667 --> 00:10:26.379
The Spanish Flu came at the end of the First World War.

94
00:10:26.379 --> 00:10:29.954
Denmark was never occupied in the First World War.

95
00:10:29.954 --> 00:10:32.617
DA is the employers' organisation.

96
00:10:32.617 --> 00:10:37.967
Women's international day of struggle is the eighth of March, from 1921.

97
00:10:37.967 --> 00:10:41.879
The Kanslergade Agreement was about the economic crisis.

98
00:10:41.879 --> 00:10:46.554
The first railway opened in 1847, in the 1840s.

99
00:10:46.554 --> 00:10:59.292
The International Workers' Association was founded in 1871, the Battle of the Fælleden took place in 1872, and the September Compromise in 1899, the root of the Danish Model.

100
00:10:59.292 --> 00:11:01.954
The Change of System came in 1901.

101
00:11:01.954 --> 00:11:09.142
The Social Liberal Party was formed in 1905, and the Conservative People's Party in 1915 to 16.

102
00:11:09.142 --> 00:11:13.542
Women's vote: 1915, in force 1918.

103
00:11:13.542 --> 00:11:17.292
The Danish West Indies were sold in 1917.

104
00:11:17.292 --> 00:11:20.992
The Spanish Flu took about fifteen thousand Danish lives.

105
00:11:20.992 --> 00:11:29.904
The Reunification was on the fifteenth of June, 1920, and the Kanslergade Agreement on the thirtieth of January, 1933.

106
00:11:29.904 --> 00:11:33.192
In Episode Four, the Germans arrive.

107
00:11:33.742 --> 00:11:36.342
Now it is time for your exam practice.

108
00:11:36.292 --> 00:11:41.204
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

109
00:11:41.204 --> 00:11:48.690
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

110
00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:50.490
Question 1.

111
00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:54.365
Was Denmark occupied by German troops during the First World War?

112
00:11:54.365 --> 00:11:55.990
Option A: Yes.

113
00:11:55.990 --> 00:11:57.660
Option B: No.
[DA] Var Danmark besat af tyske tropper under 1. verdenskrig (1914-18)?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

114
00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.210
The answer is B: No.

115
00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:12.510
It appeared in the June 2022, November 2023 and November 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ B: Nej

116
